So, here is a short JavaScript function that reverses a string using the built-in array methods, converting the string into an array of characters, reversing that array's order, and then joining the characters back together.

```javascript
function reverseString(str) {
  const tmp_chars = str.split("");
  const tmp_reversedChars = tmp_chars.reverse();
  const tmp_result = tmp_reversedChars.join("");
  return tmp_result;
}
```

This works because strings in JavaScript are not directly reversible with a single method, so the common approach is to split the string into an array using split(""), which breaks it into individual characters, then call reverse() to flip the order of that array in place, and finally call join("") to concatenate the characters back into a single string with no separator between them. Calling reverseString("hello") would therefore return "olleh".

